[Music]  benjamin netanyahu  israel's prime minister has been a  powerful force in global politics  for the past 12 years his devotees call  him the king of israel  but in recent years netanyahu has faced  repeated government collapses  and accusations of corruption bribery  and fraud  benjamin netanyahu was back before a  judge a short time ago saying he's  not guilty now the ruling coalition in  israel has collapsed  now israel is holding its fourth  election in just two years  so for him staying in the prime  minister's chair has become  something of an existential question how  will this election  mark a shift in israel's political  future  you submitted your questions to anshul  feffer the economist's jerusalem  correspondent  and greg carlstrom middle east  correspondent  here are their answers how is netanyahu  doing in the polls  the gamble that he took a few months ago  when he engineered these  early elections uh was that by the end  of march  israel would have rushed ahead with  kovitz 19 vaccinations it would have  been able to lift lockdowns and  return the economy uh to being open and  the people would be  euphoric and happy about this and that  it would pay political dividends for  netanyahu  and of course some of that has happened  that it hasn't paid the sorts of  political dividends that netanyahu hoped  it would  uh you know you look at the the polls  over the past few weeks past couple of  months  and he's sort of consistently coming in  at around 30 seats or less so a quarter  or less  of the seats in the knesset where he  would have preferred i'm sure to be  somewhere closer to at least 40 seats uh  so again yes it will come down to a  situation where  it matters whether one or two small  parties cross the threshold to make the  knesset or not it matters  whether parties pick up one extra seat  or lose one extra seat it could come  down to a very small margin  how did netanyahu remain in power after  the corruption charges  well the israeli law isn't exactly clear  on  what are the circumstances under which a  prime minister has to resign  there isn't that there are laws and  precedents under which ministers need to  resign if they're indicted  on criminal charges but since the  resignation of the prime minister would  mean  the replacement of the entire government  that is not uh required  and since netanyahu has now been  indicted on charges of bribery and fraud  has been tested and we and israel has a  serving prime minister  who is also in court facing these  charges  um you would expect the political  establishment to get together and force  out a prime minister  under these circumstances but the  israeli political status is very weak  right now  it's split into many parties netanyahu's  own party  is completely under his control and his  allies  also have an interest in him remaining  in the  imposition so it's been impossible to  force netanyahu to resign  what will happen with netanyahu's  corruption charges  if he becomes prime minister again so  13 days after the election on  april the 5th and senior is actually  scheduled to be back in court  and whatever happens in the election  that session in court  isn't it won't go away it won't  disappear but what netanyahu does hope  if he does manage to gain a majority in  the next knesset  is that he'll have finally uh enough for  supporters for a law which could  uh grant him immunity from prosecution  while  while he's serving prime minister  another thing he'd like to do is he'd  like to fire or replace  and replace the attorney general who's  brought these charges and  appointed that position someone who may  uh  decide to revisit the charges but right  now he's on track  to go back into court he's the defendant  while being the serving and perhaps also  the next prime minister right and that  goes back to this question  of why israel keeps going to election  after election it's because netanyahu if  he were to  lose power if he were to relinquish his  grip on the premiership  uh he doesn't have these avenues to try  and block or or  end the corruption trial against him he  would be just an ordinary member of  parliament he would be ineligible to  serve as a minister  and he would lose the ability to try and  steer through a law that  grants him immunity or try to remove the  attorney general  and so for him staying in the prime  minister's chair has become  something of an existential question  what explains the crisis in government  that led to this election and those in  2019 and 2020  for the last two years netanyahu despite  being the serving prime minister hasn't  had  a majority in israel's parliament the  knesset  supporting his government and that's  been a situation  also basically since the the end of 2018  and following the last three elections  the parties supporting him  have failed to gain a majority despite  the opposition in two of these elections  having  on paper a majority which supported the  challenger  in in those three elections uh benny  gantz  those parties failed to uh to  to work together partly for reasons of  the  major political diversity between them  israel's basically been in political  limbo now for two years and that's why  israel is now having its fourth election  in less than two years  what has netanyahu been like as prime  minister for the past 12 years  well i think for the past 12 years  netanyahu's continued  rule we've seen in israel an increasing  tendency  on his side towards i wouldn't say a  full autocracy but certainly  very much a presidential type of of  administration which is not israel's  uh political system is also  parliamentary system wherever netanyahu  has increasingly ignored both the  ministers of his cabinet  and his partners in coalition has really  evolved into almost a one-man rule and  we've seen it  uh over over the last 12 years and  especially in the last 12 months  during uh the coral virus pandemic in  which nathaniel has basically  run the show with a very very small  handful of advisors and without  whenever he can without advising the  cabinet you're increasingly seeing  an evolution in how politics are  conducted in a way  the entire government is revolving  around one figure around netanyahu  and i think you see that broadly not  just with israel's domestic politics but  with its foreign relations as well  the relation with the gulf states  carried out without the knowledge of  israeli ministers much of the sensitive  diplomacy with arab states is being done  by yoshi cohen the mossad director  who reports directly to the prime  minister even the relationship with  russia netanyahu and vladimir putin  speak  personally on a regular basis the  israeli foreign ministry to some extent  feeling marginalized in israel's foreign  relations at least when it comes to  some of these key relationships with  regional players and global powers  it's become very centralized under one  man  who will most likely be the next prime  minister if netanyahu loses  there are a number of challengers or  would-be prime ministers who  are hoping to replace netanyahu uh  according to the polls the one with the  at least with the biggest party after  liquid ms  lapid the centrist politician former  journalist and chat show host who's been  already for nine years in politics  trying to build uh  a base in the center ground which is  which he's succeeding in doing however  he hasn't really managed  to break beyond that sort of that block  of centrist voters and to  make to make any any headway further  into the right where most of the israeli  voters  are right now there are two other  challenges on the right there's  naftali bennett a former aide netanyahu  a former minister  in his government who who is the leader  of a smaller  right-wing party and he may even emerge  as a king maker in this election  he could he could be the one holding the  balance between a majority for the  netanyahu bloc  or for an alternative coalition so he  certainly is a is a contender for prime  minister  and the third contender is uh gideon  sarah another former liquid minister  who broke away from liquid just three  months ago and set up a party which is  basically  another liquid just like which is  against netanyahu to be another prime  minister in israel first of all  they have to work out a way to work  together  and to find a majority that will appoint  one of them  as prime minister and that's going to be  their biggest obstacle deciding which of  them  will be the leader of this new block and  of course the one person  who will absolutely not be the next  prime minister is benign who was the  main opponent  to netanyahu in the previous two  elections who ultimately after the  previous election  uh joined the unity government formed a  coalition with  netanyahu he is one of this ever  rotating caste of centrist or  center-left political figures who have  tried to challenge  netanyahu over the past decades but as  an shell said they've always  struggled to find a way to to work  together and to bring together this very  uh heterogeneous group of parties that  might like to see netanyahu out but  can't agree on much more than that how  will foreign policy change  if netanyahu loses the election  well i think if you look at certainly  the relationship with  america which is israel's most important  foreign relationship  netanyahu has had difficult relations  with democratic administrations going  back  uh decades many democrats are angry with  him not only because he was close to  donald trump but because he was openly  antagonistic towards the obama  administration also  netanyahu and democratic governments  have have not worked well together  historically and so i think in terms of  relations with america a different prime  minister  would be seen as a welcome change in  washington that being said i don't know  if it solves any of the  structural problems in the relationship  between america and israel  the next israeli prime minister whoever  it is unlikely to be  a full-throated supporter of biden's  stated policy of wanting to re-enter the  nuclear deal with iran  unlikely to do much uh vis-a-vis  diplomatic engagement with the  palestinians so  you have an influential faction within  the democratic party that  uh is quite critical of israel's policy  towards the palestinians and they won't  be mollified  i think by a change of administration in  jerusalem  could a new israeli leader find a  compromise with the palestinians  i think it's it's very striking when you  watch an israeli election campaign  because if you're  outside of israel where the  israeli-palestinian conflict  is still something that commands a lot  of attention be it in the west or in the  middle east  there's this sense or this expectation  that it might also be an issue a big  issue in israel that it should be a big  issue there  and it's not really when it comes to  electoral campaigns this is not  something that comes up people talk  about  the economy they talk about questions of  religion and state they talk  increasingly about netanyahu and the way  he governs  but the palestinians are just not an  issue that comes up on the campaign  trail and  there's not going to be much pressure  for the next israeli government whoever  is in charge of that government  uh to change policy towards the  palestinians uh and a change in  leadership  is not going to change the the  structural reasons why  there's been no progress towards any  sort of a deal  i don't see any of that changing with a  change of administration  in israel if netanyahu remains in power  how likely is annexation of the west  bank  well netanyahu promised in the past  three elections the two elections of  2019 and the one of 2020  various versions of annexation in the  west bank he spoke about  different parts of the west bank that he  would like to what he calls to extend  sovereignty to and these were  in essence uh election promises but i  don't think netanyahu ever really  intended to uh to carry carry this out  we didn't see once the elections were  over any concrete steps  towards annexation and even then though  netanyahu spoke about it and it was even  mentioned  in his coalition agreement with benny  gantz  it never happened i'm greg carlstrom  the economist middle east correspondent  thanks for watching and you can learn  more about israel's election by  clicking the link opposite don't forget  to subscribe

